Hip fracture
Geriatric & Frailty
Low-impact fall from standing height, Shortened/externally rotated leg, Inability to bear weight, Cortical disruption on plain hip radiograph

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-025
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe groin pain and a complete inability to stand following a mechanical fall at home.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a high-impact mechanism for a frail adult (fall from standing), resulting in acute, agonizing hip pain and a total inability to bear weight. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for a major long-bone fracture (neck of femur), providing immediate potent analgesia and coordinating emergency transport to a surgical facility prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I slipped in the kitchen, and now the pain in my groin is excruciating; I absolutely cannot move my leg."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the sudden onset of severe, localized deep groin and hip pain immediately following a low-impact fall from standing height on the kitchen tiles approximately two hours ago. They describe an absolute inability to bear weight on the affected limb immediately following the injury, requiring their child to drag them to a more comfortable position on the floor. They note a history of severe osteoporosis and previous fragility fractures (Colles fracture five years ago). The patient's child reports a marked reduction in baseline independent mobility prior to the fall, but the patient was previously able to walk with a frame. The patient is currently highly anxious but shows no signs of acute confusion or delirium resulting from the traumatic event.
Functional Status: Currently stranded on the floor; completely immobile due to agonizing pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme risk of hypothermia, pressure sores, and rhabdomyolysis if left on the floor; immediate surgical emergency.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/90 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Visibly pale, diaphoretic, and in severe acute distress, lying supine on the floor.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals the classic presentation of a shortened and externally rotated right lower limb while lying supine. There is exquisite, agonizing pain upon attempted active or passive internal rotation of the right hip. Palpation demonstrates localized bony tenderness over the greater trochanter. Surrounding soft tissue ecchymosis and localized swelling are beginning to develop. Distal pulses (dorsalis pedis, posterior tibial) are palpable and equal bilaterally; sensation to light touch is intact in the foot, ruling out gross neurovascular compromise. (Visible cortical disruption on plain hip radiographs is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding upon ED arrival).
Environmental Observations: A puddle of water is visible on the kitchen tiles, confirming the mechanism of the slip.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular alendronate for osteoporosis; no current anticoagulant therapy, which simplifies the imminent surgical pathway.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Fractured Neck of Femur (Right) - Status: Acute Orthopaedic Emergency
Differentials:

Pelvic Ramus Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound shortening and external rotation of the limb, which are classic hallmark signs of a femoral neck fracture rather than a stable pelvic ring injury.

Hip Dislocation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The mechanism of injury (simple low-impact fall rather than high-energy trauma like a motor vehicle accident), and the leg is externally rotated rather than the classic internal rotation seen in posterior dislocations.

Severe Soft Tissue Contusion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absolute inability to bear any weight, severe pain on passive microscopic rotation, and the gross anatomical deformity (shortening).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (Life-threatening orthopaedic emergency)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with textbook clinical signs of a fractured neck of femur following a mechanical fall: exquisite groin pain, inability to bear weight, and a shortened, externally rotated limb. Telephone triage accurately identified this as a major trauma in a frail adult. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have been catastrophic, resulting in prolonged agony, hypovolemic shock from occult bleeding into the thigh, and severe long-lie complications. This urgent face-to-face assessment confirmed the gross anatomical deformity, ruled out immediate vascular compromise, allowed for the administration of potent intramuscular analgesia, and expedited immediate emergency transport to a definitive surgical centre.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular strong opioid analgesia (e.g., morphine 5mg) immediately to manage the severe pain while awaiting transport. Immobilized the limb in the position of greatest comfort. Provided a detailed clinical handover to the responding paramedic team.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Not applicable in an acute, life-threatening trauma scenario requiring immediate transfer; focus is entirely on stabilizing and expediting transport).
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Kept the patient warm with blankets to prevent hypothermia while on the floor.
Follow-up: Immediate handover to Emergency Department Orthopaedic team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department via emergency ambulance (000); this action is currently being executed.

